<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ermanaric</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 375</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ermanaric was a king of the Greuthungi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a> in the 4th century, ruling lands north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ammianus-marcellinus">Ammianus Marcellinus</a> describes him as a warlike king who took his own life when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huns">Huns</a> attacked around 375, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jordanes">Jordanes</a> later credited him with a vast realm. He became a figure of Germanic heroic legend, appearing as Eormanric in Old English and Jörmunrekkr in Norse poetry.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The name Ermanaric is built from a Proto-Germanic root meaning 'universal' and the element for 'ruler' found in many Gothic royal names; the Gothic form is reconstructed as Airmanareiks. He is known from two Roman sources: the contemporary historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ammianus-marcellinus">Ammianus Marcellinus</a>, who calls him a most warlike king ruling wide and fertile regions, and the sixth-century Getica of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jordanes">Jordanes</a>. Ammianus says he took his own life when the Alani and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huns">Huns</a> invaded his lands in the 370s, and that a certain Vithimiris was then elected king.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jordanes">Jordanes</a> places Ermanaric over the realm of Oium, part of Scythia inhabited by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a>, and calls him a Gothic Alexander who ruled all the nations of Scythia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germania">Germania</a>. He tells how the king had a young woman, Sunilda, put to death with horses as punishment for her husband's treason; her brothers Sarus and Ammius then wounded him so badly that he could not resist the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huns">Huns</a>. Jordanes adds that he ruled successfully until his death at the age of 110.</p><p>Historians disagree on the size of his realm. Herwig Wolfram holds that it stretched from the Baltic to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> and east to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ural-mountains">Ural Mountains</a>. Peter Heather doubts that he ruled all the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a> except the Tervingi, since such an empire would be larger than any known Gothic political unit, would have left more traces, and rests on sources not reliable enough to take at face value. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-gibbon">Edward Gibbon</a> accepted the ancient accounts and credited him with subduing the Heruli, Venedi, Sclaveni, Antes and Aestii.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Ermanaric became a figure of Germanic heroic legend as Eormenric in Old English and Jǫrmunrekkr in Old Norse. Beowulf speaks of Eormenric's wiles and hatred, and the poem Deor calls him a cruel king with a wolfish mind who ruled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a> far and wide. The death of Svanhildr and his killing by Jonakr's sons recur in the Ragnarsdrápa, Hamðismál, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edda">Prose Edda</a>, the Volsunga Saga, the Gesta Danorum and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nibelungenlied">Nibelungenlied</a>. In the Thidreks Saga a treacherous counsellor, Bicke, leads him to execute his own wife, and in German poems about Dietrich of Bern he is Dietrich's uncle, who drove him into exile.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ermanaric” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the 370s the Huns crossed the Volga, defeated the Alans on the Don, absorbing many of them as allies, and then overran the Gothic realm of <b>Ermanaric</b> around 375 CE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sarmatians">Sarmatians · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Ammianus Marcellinus describes the Greuthungi as ruled by kings, and their king <b>Ermanaric</b> was remembered as a mighty warrior whose dominion took in many peoples.</blockquote><a href="/state/greuthungi">Greuthungi</a></li></ul></section>
